Operation Metro Surge is an ongoing operation by the  [LINK: (United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Immigration_and_Customs_Enforcement]  (ICE) and  [LINK: (Customs and Border Protection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Customs_and_Border_Protection]  with the stated purpose of apprehending  [LINK: (undocumented immigrants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_immigration_to_the_United_States]  and deporting them. Beginning in December 2025, it initially targeted the  [LINK: (Twin Cities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_Cities]  ( [LINK: (Minneapolis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minneapolis]  and  [LINK: (Saint Paul) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paul,_Minnesota] ), and later expanded to all of  [LINK: (Minnesota) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-3]  The  [LINK: (Department of Homeland Security) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Department_of_Homeland_Security]  called it "the largest immigration enforcement operation ever carried out". [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:9-4]  The surge has been characterized by an escalation in the severity of ICE tactics, [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-5]  harassment, and threats against observers. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:0-6]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:1-7]   It has involved the  [LINK: (detention of US citizens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deaths,_detentions_and_deportations_of_American_citizens_in_the_second_Trump_administration]  [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:28-8]  and the arrest of 3,000 people. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-nyt-2]

Federal agents killed two civilian observers during the operation:  [LINK: (Renée Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good]  and  [LINK: (Alex Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Alex_Pretti] , who were both US citizens. One person detained by ICE during the operation has  [LINK: (died while in custody) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Victor_Manuel_Diaz] . The operation has disrupted the economy and civil society of Minnesota, with schools transitioning to remote learning and immigration arrests disrupting everyday business activities. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-9]  [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-10]  Thousands in Minneapolis have protested the ICE activity. [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-11]  The governor and attorney general of Minnesota have challenged the operation, stating that its primary purpose is "retribution" instead of immigration enforcement. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-12]  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-auto-13]  On January 28, 2026, Minnesota chief US District judge  [LINK: (Patrick Schiltz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Schiltz]  found that ICE violated at least 96 court orders in Minnesota since January 1, 2026 alone. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-14]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-15]


# OVERVIEW #

One of Donald Trump's key campaign promises during his  [LINK: (second presidential campaign) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2024_presidential_campaign#Immigration]  was a crackdown on  [LINK: (illegal immigration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_immigration_to_the_United_States]  and to commence mass-deportation operations. After his  [LINK: (inauguration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_inauguration_of_Donald_Trump]  for  [LINK: (his second term) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump] , Trump signed multiple  [LINK: (executive orders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_executive_orders_in_the_second_Trump_presidency]  related to immigration in the United States, and the Department of Homeland Security and ICE agents began raids across the country. [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-16]  [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-17]

On December 4, 2025, DHS announced Operation Metro Surge, [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-18]  and on January 6, 2026, DHS announced an expansion of the effort to what it called the largest immigration enforcement operation ever carried out, [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-19]  sending 2,000 agents to the  [LINK: (Minneapolis–Saint Paul) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minneapolis%E2%80%93Saint_Paul]  metropolitan area. The surge included  [LINK: (Homeland Security Investigations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homeland_Security_Investigations]  officers focused on the  [LINK: (2020s Minnesota fraud scandals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020s_Minnesota_fraud_scandals] , as the White House announced a multiagency effort to investigate these scandals. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-20]  In addition, Donald Trump announced an effort to deport people of  [LINK: (Somali) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somali_Americans]  descent in Minnesota that he said were involved in fraudulent activity, describing them as "garbage". [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-21]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-22]  Saint Paul City Council member Molly Coleman described the first day of the action as "unlike any other day we've experienced". [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:9-4]  [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Luscombe-2026b-23]

A  [LINK: (Department of Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Justice]  attorney testified that, as of January 26, at least 2,000  [LINK: (Immigration and Customs Enforcement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration_and_Customs_Enforcement]  (ICE) officers and 1,000  [LINK: (Customs and Border Patrol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Customs_and_Border_Patrol]  officers were participating in the operation. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-24]  ICE says it has arrested 3,000 people in Minneapolis since the start of the operation. ICE detainee flights from Minneapolis more than doubled from December to January. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:16-25]

Although the effort was reputedly focused on fraud centered in the Somali-American community, only 23 arrestees were from Somalia, and none had ties to the social services frauds under investigation. Following the  [LINK: (killing of Renée Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good]  by a federal agent in January 2026, the largest fraud prosecution ( [LINK: (Feeding Our Future) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feeding_Our_Future] ) faced setbacks due to the resignation of six  [LINK: (federal prosecutors) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Attorney] , including lead attorney  [LINK: (Joe Thompson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_H._Thompson_(attorney)] . [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-26]  Persons detained on the basis of their actual or suspected immigration status have included restaurant, [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:17-27]  [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:18-28]  airport [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:19-29]  and hotel workers, [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:20-30]  Target employees, [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:21-31]  children [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-AP_News_2026-01-22-32]  and families, [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:22-33]  [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:23-34]  Native Americans, [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:24-35]  students [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-36]  and commuters. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:25-37]  Many detained individuals have been US citizens, [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:15-38]  [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:21-31]  legal residents with work authorization, [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:26-39]  [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:20-30]  or asylum seekers. [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:27-40]  The operation has seen a surge in lawsuits for wrongful detention in Minnesota. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:16-25]

Attempts by US citizens to observe or protest federal immigration raids have been met with surveillance, [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:0-6]  threats, [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:1-7]  arrests, [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:28-8]  and use of force including beatings, the use of chemical irritants, [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:29-41]  flashbangs, [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:30-42]  and  [LINK: (LRADs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LRAD] . [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-43]


# TIMELINE OF OPERATION #

At the beginning of December, ICE announced an enforcement surge in the  [LINK: (Twin Cities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minneapolis%E2%80%93Saint_Paul] . At least 12 people were arrested between December 1 and December 5. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-44]  CNN reported the operations were set to be primarily focused on undocumented  [LINK: (Somali immigrants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Somalis_in_Minneapolis%E2%80%93Saint_Paul] . [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-45]  Border Patrol official  [LINK: (Gregory Bovino) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_Bovino]  requested identification from employees of an auto repair business after the owner, a US citizen who had fled  [LINK: (Somalia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somalia] , advised a man that he didn't have to answer their questions. [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-46]  Minneapolis mayor  [LINK: (Jacob Frey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Frey]  signed an executive order banning federal officials from using city property for staging areas. [LINK: ([47]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-47]  In late December, ICE agents threatened a pair of observers with arrest, then drove to the home of one of the observers and photographed it. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:0-6]

December 6 – Around a dozen federal agents entered a home in  [LINK: (Burnsville, Minnesota) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burnsville,_Minnesota] , and arrested four people, including the parents of a seven-year-old boy. They were taken to detention facilities outside of the state. [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:23-34]

December 9 – A 55-year-old Minneapolis resident and US citizen was detained by federal immigration agents while observing an ICE enforcement action on a public street in north Minneapolis. According to local news reporting, civil rights organizations, and court filings, the woman drove to the scene after receiving alerts about ongoing federal activity and stood on a sidewalk near the enforcement site. Within seconds of asking an ICE agent "Are you ICE?", she was reportedly tackled to the ground, handcuffed, and taken into custody by agents. [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-48]  Reporting indicates she was transported to the  [LINK: (Whipple Federal Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whipple_Federal_Building]  in Fort Snelling, shackled, and held in a cell for approximately four to five hours before being released without charges. During her detention, parts of her clothing and her wedding ring were reportedly removed. The ACLU complaint asserts that the observer was on a public street, did not interfere with federal agents, and that the detention was part of a broader pattern of confrontations between ICE agents and individuals documenting federal actions. [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-49]

December 10 – A 20-year-old US citizen in the  [LINK: (Cedar-Riverside neighborhood) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedar-Riverside,_Minneapolis]  in Minneapolis was wrongfully detained by unidentified ICE agents during his lunch break. The man was tackled, put into a headlock and taken in a vehicle to the Whipple Building, despite offering to show his passport by shouting "I'm a citizen. I'm a citizen." upon contact with the agents. He was released after being allowed to show his passport hours later and walked back to the Cedar-Riverside neighborhood in the snow. Minneapolis and Minnesota police and politicians denounced his detention as unlawful and unconstitutional. [LINK: ([50]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-50]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-51]

December 14 – In an interview, Representative  [LINK: (Ilhan Omar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ilhan_Omar]  said that her son had been pulled over by ICE. He was able to show the agents his passport and was not detained. [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-52]

December 15 – ICE agents in Minneapolis attempted to arrest a woman who they said had attempted to vandalize their vehicle. The use of force in detaining the woman was criticized by Minneapolis police chief  [LINK: (Brian O'Hara) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_O%27Hara] , and led to pushback from bystanders, who surrounded the agents and threw snowballs at them until they abandoned the arrest. [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-53]

December 22 – ICE agents opened fire on a Cuban immigrant who they alleged had hit them with an SUV while fleeing arrest in Saint Paul. [LINK: ([54]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-54]

January 6
DHS announced it was launching what it called the largest immigration enforcement operation ever carried out, sending 2,000 agents to the Twin Cities. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:9-4] 
ICE agents photographed the license plates and faces of a St. Paul couple observing their activities, then greeted them by name and drove to their house.  According to the couple, the encounter was intimidating and left them shaken. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:0-6] 
A woman, her child and her neighbor's child, who is African American, were also pulled over by ICE. Observers gathered around the traffic stop, and the agents eventually left without making an arrest or speaking to anyone in the car. [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-55] 
A 10-year-old Columbia Heights student and her mother were taken by ICE and sent to a Texas detention center. [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:4-56] 
Victor Manuel Diaz is taken by ICE and transferred within a week to  [LINK: (Camp East Montana) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camp_East_Montana] , a detention center at  [LINK: (Fort Bliss, Texas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Bliss,_Texas] . [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-VictorMD-AP-57]

DHS announced it was launching what it called the largest immigration enforcement operation ever carried out, sending 2,000 agents to the Twin Cities. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:9-4]

ICE agents photographed the license plates and faces of a St. Paul couple observing their activities, then greeted them by name and drove to their house.  According to the couple, the encounter was intimidating and left them shaken. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:0-6]

A woman, her child and her neighbor's child, who is African American, were also pulled over by ICE. Observers gathered around the traffic stop, and the agents eventually left without making an arrest or speaking to anyone in the car. [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-55]

