January 18
Without a search warrant, federal immigration agents made entry into a US citizen's home in Minnesota, handcuffed him, and took him outside in freezing temperatures in his underwear. He was detained for two hours before immigration agents released him. [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:15-38]  ICE officials claimed they were searching for two men with criminal records who they believed were living in the house. Local media reported that one of the men ICE claimed they were looking for had been in a Minnesota prison since 2024. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:11-85] 
ICE officially announces the death of Victor Manuel Diaz while in ICE custody. In a statement, ICE claims that Diaz committed suicide, which the family disagrees with. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-VictorMD-AP-57]  [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-ICE-Manuel-Diaz-86]

Without a search warrant, federal immigration agents made entry into a US citizen's home in Minnesota, handcuffed him, and took him outside in freezing temperatures in his underwear. He was detained for two hours before immigration agents released him. [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:15-38]  ICE officials claimed they were searching for two men with criminal records who they believed were living in the house. Local media reported that one of the men ICE claimed they were looking for had been in a Minnesota prison since 2024. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:11-85]

ICE officially announces the death of Victor Manuel Diaz while in ICE custody. In a statement, ICE claims that Diaz committed suicide, which the family disagrees with. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-VictorMD-AP-57]  [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-ICE-Manuel-Diaz-86]

January 19
ICE arrested a man working at a St. Louis Park hotel where agents were staying. The man came to the United States as a refugee and had a valid work permit, and was ordered released by a judge on January 25. [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:20-30]

ICE arrested a man working at a St. Louis Park hotel where agents were staying. The man came to the United States as a refugee and had a valid work permit, and was ordered released by a judge on January 25. [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:20-30]

January 20
A police chief shared that two off-duty  [LINK: (Saint Paul Police) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paul_Police_Department]  officers had been briefly stopped by ICE. [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:3-87] 
Brooklyn Park Police Chief Brian O'Hara stated that an off-duty Brooklyn Park Police Department officer had been stopped by ICE at gunpoint. [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:3-87] 
 [LINK: (Liam Conejo Ramos) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Conejo_Ramos] , a five-year-old student at  [LINK: (Valley View Elementary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_Heights,_Minnesota#Independent_School_District_13]  was approached by masked ICE agents as he returned home from school with his father. According to school officials, they took the boy to the door of the house and used him as 'bait' [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-MPR_News_2026-01-21-88]  [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-washpost_2026-0122-89]  to get the residents to open the door. In response to backlash, senior ICE officials alleged the officers were attempting to protect the child from the cold. [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-BBC_News_2026-01-22-90]  At the same time, ICE claimed that Liam's mother was inside the house. School officials suggested the pregnant [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-CNN_2026-01-22-91]  mother was likely fearful of opening the door. [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-HuffPost_2026-01-22-92]  The ICE agents took Liam and his father away to a detention center in Texas. According to their lawyer, the family came to the US in 2024 from  [LINK: (Ecuador) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecuador]  and has an active asylum claim. [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-nytimes_2026-0122-93]  [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-AP_News_2026-01-22-32]  This was the fourth student at the Columbia Heights School District to be detained by ICE. Earlier the same day, a 17-year-old student was taken from his car by ICE agents. [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:4-56]

A police chief shared that two off-duty  [LINK: (Saint Paul Police) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paul_Police_Department]  officers had been briefly stopped by ICE. [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:3-87]

Brooklyn Park Police Chief Brian O'Hara stated that an off-duty Brooklyn Park Police Department officer had been stopped by ICE at gunpoint. [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:3-87]

[LINK: (Liam Conejo Ramos) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liam_Conejo_Ramos] , a five-year-old student at  [LINK: (Valley View Elementary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_Heights,_Minnesota#Independent_School_District_13]  was approached by masked ICE agents as he returned home from school with his father. According to school officials, they took the boy to the door of the house and used him as 'bait' [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-MPR_News_2026-01-21-88]  [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-washpost_2026-0122-89]  to get the residents to open the door. In response to backlash, senior ICE officials alleged the officers were attempting to protect the child from the cold. [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-BBC_News_2026-01-22-90]  At the same time, ICE claimed that Liam's mother was inside the house. School officials suggested the pregnant [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-CNN_2026-01-22-91]  mother was likely fearful of opening the door. [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-HuffPost_2026-01-22-92]  The ICE agents took Liam and his father away to a detention center in Texas. According to their lawyer, the family came to the US in 2024 from  [LINK: (Ecuador) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecuador]  and has an active asylum claim. [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-nytimes_2026-0122-93]  [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-AP_News_2026-01-22-32]  This was the fourth student at the Columbia Heights School District to be detained by ICE. Earlier the same day, a 17-year-old student was taken from his car by ICE agents. [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:4-56]

January 21
Fong Khang, a US legal permanent resident from Laos, was taken into ICE custody and transferred to Texas in apparent violation of a federal court order. The day before the  [LINK: (Minnesota Board of Pardons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_Board_of_Pardons]  had set aside Khang's criminal convictions; he had remained free of convictions since 2010. According to his lawyer he was to be returned to Minnesota. [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-94] 
Volunteers delivering food to migrants reported ICE vans were staking out area food banks and following them. Visits to local food pantries were down 50–80 percent. [LINK: ([95]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-95]

