January 28
ICE detains a volunteer community food shelf delivery driver at the Hallie Q. Brown Community Center in St. Paul, in violation of a mayoral directive forbidding immigration enforcement on city-owned property. ICE cited the man's criminal record as a teenager to justify his detention and deportation. Since his release from prison in 2016, the man had been involved in various community organizations in  [LINK: (Frogtown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frogtown,_Saint_Paul,_Minnesota]  and St. Paul's North End, and community members spoke to his good character. [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-100]

ICE detains a volunteer community food shelf delivery driver at the Hallie Q. Brown Community Center in St. Paul, in violation of a mayoral directive forbidding immigration enforcement on city-owned property. ICE cited the man's criminal record as a teenager to justify his detention and deportation. Since his release from prison in 2016, the man had been involved in various community organizations in  [LINK: (Frogtown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frogtown,_Saint_Paul,_Minnesota]  and St. Paul's North End, and community members spoke to his good character. [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-100]

January 30 and 31
A second Twin Cities general strike planned by  [LINK: (University of Minnesota) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Minnesota]  (UMN) campus student union organizers [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-101]  expands nationally, branded as National Days of Action in Solidarity, calls for a general strike and economic blackout - no school, work or shopping. [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-102]  [LINK: ([103]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-103]

A second Twin Cities general strike planned by  [LINK: (University of Minnesota) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Minnesota]  (UMN) campus student union organizers [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-101]  expands nationally, branded as National Days of Action in Solidarity, calls for a general strike and economic blackout - no school, work or shopping. [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-102]  [LINK: ([103]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-103]


# SHOOTINGS #

On January 7, 2026,  [LINK: (Renée Nicole Macklin Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good] , a 37-year-old American woman, was fatally shot in  [LINK: (Minneapolis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minneapolis] , Minnesota, by  [LINK: (United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Immigration_and_Customs_Enforcement]  (ICE) agent Jonathan Ross. [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-107]  Good was in her car, stopped sideways in the street when Ross walked around it and then walked back and around her vehicle. Other agents approached, and one ordered her to get out of the car while reaching through her open window. Good briefly reversed, then began moving forward and to the right, into the direction of traffic. At this point, Ross was standing at the front-left of the vehicle and fired three shots, killing her, as her vehicle passed him, turning away from him. The killing sparked national protests and multiple investigations.

[LINK: (Federal law enforcement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_law_enforcement_in_the_United_States]  officials and President  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump]  defended the shooting, saying the agent acted in  [LINK: (self-defense) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-defense] , that Good ran him over, and that the agent was recovering in a hospital. Their accounts of the shooting were contested by eyewitnesses, journalists, [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Renée_Good_Lum-2026b-108]  and  [LINK: (Democratic Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_(United_States)]  lawmakers, some of whom called for criminal proceedings against Ross. [LINK: ([108]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Renée_Good_Barnett-2026-109]  [LINK: ([109]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Renée_Good_The_Guardian-2026-110]  The president and federal officials were criticized for espousing conclusions before any investigation had occurred. Minneapolis mayor  [LINK: (Jacob Frey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Frey]  and Minnesota governor  [LINK: (Tim Walz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tim_Walz]  called on ICE to end their presence in the city.

The killing sparked widespread  [LINK: (protests in Minneapolis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good_protests] , [LINK: ([110]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Renée_Good_Mitchell-2026-111]  and other US cities including Chicago, New York City, Los Angeles, San Francisco, Seattle, and Washington, D.C. [LINK: ([111]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Renée_Good_Minnesota_Public_Radio-2026-112]  Marches in Minneapolis prompted the closing of public schools and the deployment of more police officers. Federal agents used  [LINK: (tear gas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tear_gas]  and  [LINK: (pepper spray) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pepper_spray]  against protesters, and Governor Walz placed the  [LINK: (National Guard) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_National_Guard]  on standby.

Leaders of the Justice Department's  [LINK: (Civil Rights Division) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Justice_Civil_Rights_Division]  declined to open a constitutional investigation, which led more than a dozen  [LINK: (federal prosecutors) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assistant_United_States_Attorney]  in Minneapolis and Washington to resign in protest. Minnesota attorney general Keith Ellison, along with the cities of Minneapolis and Saint Paul, filed suit against the  [LINK: (Department of Homeland Security) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Homeland_Security]  (DHS) to halt ICE deployments. The incident intensified national debate over immigration enforcement and renewed calls to  [LINK: (abolish ICE) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abolish_ICE] .

On January 14, 2026, Julio Cesar Sosa-Celis, a  [LINK: (Venezuelan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venezuela]  man, [LINK: ([112]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:12-113]  was shot in the leg by a US  [LINK: (Immigration and Customs Enforcement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration_and_Customs_Enforcement]  (ICE) agent in Minneapolis. [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting-114]  [LINK: ([114]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-speaksout-115]  The shooting took place in the  [LINK: (north Minneapolis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Minneapolis]  area. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:10-70]  According to the Department of Homeland Security, there was a car chase and then a struggle with a federal agent in front of a residence, where two other people attacked the officer. [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting-114]  [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting2-116]  The agent shot Sosa-Celis, who went inside the residence and refused to come out. Federal agents went inside the residence. [LINK: ([112]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:12-113]  [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting-114]  [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting2-116]  Sosa-Celis was transported to a hospital. [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting-114]  [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting2-116]  Protests developed near the scene, with federal agents firing tear gas and protesters throwing rocks and fireworks. [LINK: ([116]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:13-117]  Following the shooting, Minneapolis mayor  [LINK: (Jacob Frey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Frey]  said the ICE deployment to Minneapolis was "not sustainable" and was putting Minneapolis in an "impossible situation", and he called for protests to be peaceful. [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting-114]  [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-icehomeshooting2-116]  [LINK: ([114]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-speaksout-115]

In an affidavit filed in federal court January 16, a  [LINK: (Federal Bureau of Investigation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Bureau_of_Investigation]  (FBI) agent gave an account of the incident that varied in several details from the government's first account. According to the FBI affidavit, ICE agents identified the license plate of a car as belonging to a man their records showed had unlawfully entered the US. They identified the driver, who said he had recently purchased the car, as the man they were looking for although the driver was 50 pounds heavier and five inches taller than ICE's suspect; both had short brown hair. Sosa-Celis, the man who was shot and who ICE said was the target of the stop, was not in the car. The driver fled the stop, crashing into a light pole near the house where Sosa-Celis was standing on the porch. The ICE agent, who had not been identified, caught the driver in the yard and an altercation ensued between the two. Sosa-Celis tried to pull the driver away from the ICE agent; as the ICE agent drew his pistol both fled toward the house and Sosa-Celis was shot. The ICE agent reported a "bloody gash" to his hand. The third man supposedly involved in the altercation was not mentioned in the FBI affidavit nor could it be confirmed that he was at the scene. [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-118]

On January 24, 2026,  [LINK: (Alex Jeffrey Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Alex_Pretti] , [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-119]  a 37-year-old American  [LINK: (intensive care nurse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intensive_care_nurse]  for the  [LINK: (United States Department of Veterans Affairs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Veterans_Affairs] , was shot multiple times and killed by  [LINK: (United States Customs and Border Protection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Customs_and_Border_Protection]  agents in  [LINK: (Minneapolis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minneapolis] , Minnesota. The incident occurred amid widespread protests against Operation Metro Surge following the  [LINK: (killing of Renée Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good]  on January 7 by a  [LINK: (United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Immigration_and_Customs_Enforcement]  agent.

Pretti was filming law enforcement agents with his phone and directing traffic. At one point, he stood between an agent and a woman whom the agent had pushed to the ground, putting his arm around the woman. [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_auto-120]  He was then  [LINK: (pepper-sprayed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pepper-spray]  and wrestled to the ground by several federal agents, with around six surrounding him when he was shot and killed. [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_NguyenMJ-121]  [LINK: ([121]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_StarTVideos-122]  [LINK: ([122]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_DropSiteNews-123]  Bystander video verified and reviewed by  [LINK: (Reuters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reuters] , the  [LINK: (BBC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC] ,  [LINK: (The Wall Street Journal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wall_Street_Journal] , and the  [LINK: (Associated Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press]  (AP) appears to show an agent removing a gun and moving away from Pretti roughly one second before another agent fires at him. [LINK: ([123]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_WSJ2026-01-25-124]  [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_EvansReuters-125]  [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_Vinick-126]  [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_BBC-127]  AP reported that a voice can be heard saying "gun, gun" right before the first shot. [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-128]

Pretti was legally licensed to carry a handgun. [LINK: ([128]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_MackeyGuardian-129]  In reviewing video evidence, Reuters, the BBC,  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times] ,  [LINK: (CNN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNN] , and  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  all concluded that he was holding a cell phone, not a gun, in the moments before being tackled and pinned to the ground. [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_EvansReuters-125]  [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_BBC-127]  [LINK: ([129]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_Lum-2026-130]  [LINK: ([130]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_CNN-Trum-claims-131]  Agents appear to have shot at him at least ten times within five seconds, continuing after he lay motionless. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_BBC-127]  [LINK: ([129]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_Lum-2026-130]  [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_Vinick-126]  A civilian recounted how nearly two dozen witnesses to the shooting were taken to and detained at the federally-controlled  [LINK: (Whipple Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whipple_Federal_Building]  for hours before being released. [LINK: ([131]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-132]  As with the Renée Good case, state investigators were denied access to the shooting scene by the federal government. [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_Kare11-133]

The  [LINK: (Trump administration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump]  defended the shooting, though many of its claims were contradicted by video evidence and witness testimony. [LINK: ([133]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_BarnettStar-134]  The shooting accelerated  [LINK: (ongoing protests against US immigration forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_against_mass_deportation_during_the_second_Trump_administration]  locally and nationally. [LINK: ([134]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Killing_of_Alex_Pretti_AmatulliGuardian-135]  The killing and the government's defense provoked widespread criticism, including from  [LINK: (Republicans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_(United_States)] , forcing Trump to attempt a course correction. This move has been viewed with skepticism by local activists, who expect continued immigration enforcement in the region. [LINK: ([135]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-136]  Comments by Trump administration officials denouncing Pretti's possession of a firearm were condemned by  [LINK: (gun rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gun_rights]  groups such as the  [LINK: (National Rifle Association) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Rifle_Association]  and  [LINK: (Gun Owners of America) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gun_Owners_of_America]  citing his rights under the  [LINK: (Second Amendment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Amendment] . [LINK: ([136]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-137]


# WARRANTLESS SEARCHES AND ARRESTS #

An  [LINK: (internal ICE memo from May 2025) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deportation_in_the_second_Trump_administration#Administrative_warrants]  asserts that ICE officers have the authority to  [LINK: (forcibly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Use_of_force]  enter homes of those subject to  [LINK: (removal orders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Removal_proceedings]  with an  [LINK: (administrative warrant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_Immigration_Reform_and_Immigrant_Responsibility_Act_of_1996#Administrative_warrants] , rather than a  [LINK: (judicial warrant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warrant_(law)] , allowing for  [LINK: (search and seizure) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Search_and_seizure]  without approval from a  [LINK: (federal district judge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_federal_judge]  or  [LINK: (federal magistrate judge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_magistrate_judge] . According to a whistleblower, ICE trainees are taught to follow the memo's guidance instead of training materials which contradict the memo. [LINK: ([137]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-138]

On January 11, federal immigration agents arrested a  [LINK: (Liberian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberia]  immigrant after breaking into his home with a  [LINK: (battering ram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battering_ram]  despite only having an administrative warrant issued by an immigration officer and not a judicial warrant, and despite the fact that he had regular meetings with immigration authorities for years prior to his arrest. [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-139]  [LINK: ([139]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-140]  On January 15,  [LINK: (Minnesota US District Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_District_Court_for_the_District_of_Minnesota]  judge  [LINK: (Jeffrey Bryan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeffrey_Bryan]  ruled that the forced entry into the Liberian immigrant's home constituted a  [LINK: (warrantless search) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warrantless_searches_in_the_United_States]  in violation of the  [LINK: (Fourth Amendment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourth_Amendment_to_the_United_States_Constitution]  and ordered his release. [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-141]  However, ICE detained the Liberian immigrant a second time only a day later when he and his attorney attended a subsequent routine check-in at a federal building. [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-142]  [LINK: ([142]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-143]

On January 18, a  [LINK: (Hmong American) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hmong_Americans]  citizen was mistakenly arrested by ICE agents after they forced entry into his home  [LINK: (without presenting any warrant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrest_without_warrant] ; [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:15-38]  [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-144]  the target of the search has reportedly been in prison since September 2024. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:11-85]  [LINK: ([144]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-145]  In a review of 33  [LINK: (wrongful detention) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrongful_detention]  lawsuits filed in the Minnesota US District Court on January 16 and January 17, the  [LINK: (Minnesota Star Tribune) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_Star_Tribune]  found that there was no evidence of a warrant in the majority of the lawsuits. [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-MN_Star_Tribune_1-22-2026-146]


# RESPONSES #

Minnesota Attorney General  [LINK: (Keith Ellison) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keith_Ellison]  described the federal immigration enforcement deployment as "in essence, a federal invasion of the Twin Cities and Minnesota, and it must stop" asserting that the thousands of armed ICE and DHS agents had caused serious harm and chaos under the guise of immigration enforcement. He criticized the operation in a press conference on January 12 as not helpful: "This surge has made us less safe. Thousands of poorly trained, aggressive, and armed agents of the federal government have rolled into our communities. They have fired chemical irritants at people obeying lawful orders. This is an unlawful commandeering of police resources." [LINK: ([146]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-147]  [LINK: ([147]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-148]  [LINK: ([148]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-149] [ [LINK: (independent source needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Independent_sources] ]

On January 15, Minnesota Governor  [LINK: (Tim Walz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tim_Walz]  insisted that the Trump administration "stop this campaign of retribution". [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-auto-13]  After the  [LINK: (killing of Alex Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Alex_Pretti] , Walz likened the impact of federal immigration enforcement in Minnesota to the  [LINK: (experiences of Anne Frank) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_Frank]  during the  [LINK: (Holocaust) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Holocaust] . Walz stated that like Frank, many children were hiding in their homes and afraid to leave due to the ongoing immigration actions in the state. The  [LINK: (US Holocaust Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Holocaust_Memorial_Museum]  later criticized his comparison of Franks experiences and those of immigrant children, stating that Frank was targeted and murdered solely for being Jewish, and any false equivalences are not acceptable especially while  [LINK: (antisemitism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antisemitism]  levels increase. [LINK: ([149]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-150]  [LINK: ([150]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-151]

On January 16, Minneapolis Mayor  [LINK: (Jacob Frey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Frey]  criticized the broader operation, stating it was "not normal immigration enforcement" and calling on the federal government to halt "unconstitutional conduct that is invading our streets each and every day". Regarding discrimination of specific groups, he added "We have seen consistent unconstitutional practice by ICE discriminating only on the basis of are you Latino, are you Somali". [LINK: ([151]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-152]  [LINK: ([152]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-153]

Republican Chris Mandel dropped out of the  [LINK: (2026 Minnesota gubernatorial election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Minnesota_gubernatorial_election]  saying it was because of the Republicans' handling of immigration enforcement in his state.  He said on X:  "I cannot support the national Republicans' stated retribution on the citizens of our state, nor can I count myself a member of a party that would do so..."  [LINK: ([153]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-154]

Vice President  [LINK: (JD Vance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JD_Vance]  defended the ICE agent involved in the  [LINK: (killing of Renée Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good]  and rejected claims of unlawful actions by federal agents, remarking that characterizations of Good as an innocent civilian were "a lie" and that the officer was acting in self-defense. [LINK: ([154]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-155]

After Alex Pretti's killing, President  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump]  spoke with Walz by phone about the operation. [LINK: ([155]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-156]  Trump subsequently announced that he would be sending White House Border Czar  [LINK: (Tom Homan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_Homan]  to Minnesota to oversee the operation. [LINK: ([97]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-Poli-Ja26-97]  [LINK: ([156]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-157]  Border Patrol official  [LINK: (Gregory Bovino) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_Bovino]  was reportedly expected to leave the state with some agents. [LINK: ([157]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-158]  [LINK: ([158]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-159]  Trump blamed Democrats for the killings of Pretti and Renée Good, arguing that they had encouraged obstruction of law enforcement operations. [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-160]

On January 8, a day after the  [LINK: (killing of Renée Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good] , representative  [LINK: (Robin Kelly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robin_Kelly]  (IL-D) announced plans to introduce articles of impeachment against Kristi Noem; [LINK: ([160]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-161]  the articles were formally introduced on January 15. [LINK: ([161]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-162]  By January 26, days after the  [LINK: (killing of Alex Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Alex_Pretti] , the articles had 140 Democratic cosponsors. [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-163]

On January 22, 2026, the House passed an appropriations package that included funding for the Department of Homeland Security (DHS), including ICE. [LINK: ([163]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-164]   On the same day, Democratic lawmakers sent a letter to Kristi Noem saying they are outraged by 53 deaths in ICE/CBP custody and accusing DHS of a "callous disregard for human life". [LINK: ([164]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-165]  Hours after the January 24 killing of Alex Pretti, Senate minority leader  [LINK: (Chuck Schumer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Schumer]  (NY-D) said that Senate Democrats would not pass appropriations that included the DHS funding; support from Senate Democrats is necessary to pass the bill. If appropriations are not passed by January 30, the government will enter a  [LINK: (partial government shutdown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_shutdown] . [LINK: ([165]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-166]   [LINK: (Susan Collins) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susan_Collins]  (ME-R), chair of the  [LINK: (Senate Appropriations Committee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senate_Appropriations_Committee] , said that Republicans are open to reforms for DHS but opposed a separated DHS funding measure; she said the current bill included items such as increased DHS oversight and $20 million for  [LINK: (body cameras) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_body_camera] . [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-167]

On January 29, eight Republicans joined all the Democrats in the Senate to block the government spending bill that would fund DHS over concerns about immigration enforcement following the killing of Alex Pretti.  Democrats said they would not approve the spending bill that includes DHS "[u]ntil ICE is properly reined in and overhauled legislatively." [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-168]  [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-169]

On January 9, Minneapolis and  [LINK: (Fridley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fridley,_Minnesota]  schools were temporarily closed after reports of ICE agents tackling people at Roosevelt High School a day before. [LINK: ([175]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-176]  In the evening, over 1000 protesters gathered outside the Canopy by  [LINK: (Hilton hotel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilton_Worldwide#Lodging_of_ICE_officers]  in downtown Minneapolis where ICE officers were believed to have been staying. Police Chief  [LINK: (Brian O'Hara) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_O%27Hara]  described it as a "noise protest" to disrupt those inside until protesters began causing property damage and one police officer was injured by thrown ice. At 10:15 pm police declared the protest an unlawful assembly and 30 people were arrested, at which point the crowd dispersed. [LINK: ([176]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-List_of_Renée_Good_protests_:15-177]  [LINK: ([177]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-178]  [LINK: ([178]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-179]

On January 10, protests continued with thousands assembling at  [LINK: (Powderhorn Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powderhorn_Park_(urban_park)] . [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-180]  [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-181]  In the evening,  [LINK: (Reuters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reuters]  and  [LINK: (MS NOW) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MS_NOW]  estimated that number as "tens of thousands." [LINK: ([181]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-182]  [LINK: ([182]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-183]

On January 23, more than 700 small businesses and several cultural institutions closed as part of an economic protest and  [LINK: (general strike) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_general_strike] . Organizers estimated that 50,000 attended the associated protests in subzero temperature. [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-184]  [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-185]  In the morning of the same day, hundreds of clergy members protested at  [LINK: (Minneapolis–Saint Paul International Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minneapolis%E2%80%93Saint_Paul_International_Airport]  calling for an end to the ICE surge. Around 100 clergy members were arrested during the protests. [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-186]  [LINK: ([186]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-187]

On January 24, in the aftermath of the  [LINK: (killing of Alex Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Alex_Pretti] , hundreds gathered at Whittier Park to protest. A vigil was held at Whittier Park and the intersection of 27th and Nicollet Avenue. [LINK: ([187]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-188]

On January 25, about 1,000 people gathered in about 3 °F (−16 °C) weather [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-189]  in  [LINK: (Government Plaza) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_Plaza_station]  outside the  [LINK: (Hennepin County Government Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hennepin_County_Government_Center]  in downtown Minneapolis for a rally, protesting ICE and CBP and calling for justice after the  [LINK: (killings of Renée Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good]  and  [LINK: (Alex Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Alex_Pretti] . Passing cars honked in support of the protesters. After the rally, the crowd marched down 3rd Ave. and Washington Ave., chanting phrases such as "no more Minnesota nice, Minneapolis will strike," "shut it down, shut it down, shut it down," and "strike, strike, strike, strike." [LINK: ([189]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-190]

Following the distribution of an anonymous flier online that evening, demonstrators gathered outside a Home2 Suites hotel on University Ave SE that ICE agents were allegedly staying at. [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-191]  Demonstrators created noise and vandalized the exterior of the building. According to local police, federal agents arrived without notifying them as they were attempting to issue dispersal orders and deployed tear gas. [LINK: ([191]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-192]

On January 30, There is a planned strike, attempting to replicate and expand on the previous January 26 strike. [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-193]  The strike organizers are calling for a total economic blackout, Including schooling, work, and shopping. Though schooling in particular is being focused on, with campus protests being a focal point in the movement. [LINK: ([193]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-194]  The activist groups are also hoping to expand to a national blackout, not just local to Minnesota. [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-195]

Minneapolis  [LINK: (labor unions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_union]  and community organizations called for a January 23  [LINK: (general strike) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_strike]  in response to the ICE surge. [LINK: ([195]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-196]  The name of the strike is "ICE Out of MN: Day of Truth and Freedom." [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-197]  On January 23, thousands of Minnesotans participated in the strike against ICE actions in their state. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:5-198]  In the Minneapolis-Saint Paul area, hundreds of businesses closed to protest Operation Metro Surge. [LINK: ([198]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-199]  Businesses across the state also closed in solidarity. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:5-198]  State  [LINK: (museums) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum]  were also closed. [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:6-200]

Dozens of  [LINK: (priests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Priest]  and clergy members were arrested during their protest at the  [LINK: (Minneapolis–Saint Paul International Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minneapolis%E2%80%93Saint_Paul_International_Airport] . [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:5-198]  Despite frigid weather, [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:7-201]  in Minneapolis,  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  reported that "tens of thousands" of protesters marched through the streets. [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:6-200]  The march began at 2:00 pm and started at The Commons, located near  [LINK: (US Bank Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Bank_Stadium] . [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:8-202]  The march ended at the  [LINK: (Target Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Target_Center] . [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:8-202]

Over a thousand labor unions endorsed the general strike, including the Minnesota  [LINK: (AFL-CIO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AFL-CIO] . [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:6-200]  The strike was also endorsed by the Minneapolis city council. [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:6-200]  It may be one of the state's largest strikes. [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:7-201]

The day after the Jan. 23 strike, VA nurse Alex Pretti was shot and killed by CBP. A second strike was organized for Friday, Jan. 30, with protests and vigils on Saturday, Jan. 31. [LINK: ([202]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-203]

By January 15, Minneapolis church Dios Habla Hoy had delivered over 12,000 boxes of food in six weeks to families in hiding during the operation. [LINK: ([203]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-204]

On January 28,  [LINK: (Bruce Springsteen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruce_Springsteen]  released the protest song " [LINK: (Streets of Minneapolis") -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Streets_of_Minneapolis] , condemning the violence of federal agents in the city. [LINK: ([204]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-205]  The video version was released on January 29. [LINK: ([205]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-206]  [LINK: ([206]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-207]

On January 25, an open letter was posted to the Minnesota Chamber of Commerce website signed by over 60  [LINK: (CEOs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer]  of Minnesota-based companies calling for an "immediate deescalation of tensions". Signers included the CEOs of  [LINK: (3M) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3M] ,  [LINK: (Cargill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cargill] ,  [LINK: (Mayo Clinic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayo_Clinic] ,  [LINK: (Target) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Target_Corporation] ,  [LINK: (Best Buy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Best_Buy] ,  [LINK: (UnitedHealth Group) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UnitedHealth_Group] , and  [LINK: (General Mills) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Mills] . [LINK: ([207]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-208]

In response to ICE actions in Minneapolis, officials in  [LINK: (Italy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy]  expressed concern over the reported planned involvement of ICE in providing security for US officials during the  [LINK: (2026 Winter Olympic Games) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Winter_Olympic_Games] . [LINK: ([208]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-209]


# LAWSUITS #

On 12 January, the state governments of Minnesota and Illinois and the city governments of Minneapolis and Saint Paul filed federal lawsuits against the US Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and top federal officials, including the heads of ICE and Customs and Border Protection (CBP). [LINK: ([209]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-210]  [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-211]  They argued that the large-scale deployment of ICE agents is unconstitutional, unlawful, and has disrupted civic life and violated civil liberties. [LINK: ([211]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:14-212]  The State of Minnesota invokes the  [LINK: (Tenth Amendment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenth_Amendment_to_the_United_States_Constitution] , arguing that the unilateral deployment of federal agents to perform general policing duties constitutes an unconstitutional commandeering of state resources and a violation of the state's sovereign police powers. [LINK: ([212]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-213]  The City of Minneapolis challenges the operation under the  [LINK: (Administrative Procedure Act) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Administrative_Procedure_Act]  (APA), contending that the sudden designation of schools and hospitals as enforcement zones was an "arbitrary and capricious" policy change made without the required public notice or comment period. [LINK: ([213]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-214]  [LINK: ([211]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:14-212]  On January 19, the Justice Department filed a request to reject lawsuit's motion for a preliminary injunction and temporary restraining order. [LINK: ([214]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-215]  Oral arguments began being held in the case on January 26. [LINK: ([215]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-AP-Ja26-216]  At least 20  [LINK: (state attorneys general) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_attorney_general]  have filed amicus briefs in favor of Minnesota. [LINK: ([216]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-217]

On January 31, Minnesota US District Court judge  [LINK: (Katherine M. Menendez) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katherine_M._Menendez]  denied a preliminary injunction requested by the plaintiffs against federal government, arguing that the "the relative merits of each side's competing positions are unclear" that weighed against approval. [LINK: ([217]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-218]  [LINK: ([218]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-219]

In a letter to Minnesota Governor Tim Walz dated January 24, Attorney General  [LINK: (Pam Bondi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pam_Bondi]  requested that the state government repeal  [LINK: (sanctuary policies in the state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_city#Minnesota] , give the  [LINK: (Justice Department's Civil Rights Division access to the state's voter rolls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_efforts_to_disrupt_voting_after_the_2024_United_States_presidential_election#Voter_roll_data_requests] , and share its  [LINK: (Medicaid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medicaid] ,  [LINK: (Food and Nutrition Service) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Food_and_Nutrition_Service] , and  [LINK: (Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supplemental_Nutrition_Assistance_Program]  records with the Justice Department for its investigation of the  [LINK: (2020s Minnesota fraud scandals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020s_Minnesota_fraud_scandals] . [LINK: ([219]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-220]  Bondi asserted that this was part of an effort to "restore the order of law, support ICE officers and bring an end to the chaos in Minnesota." [LINK: ([220]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-221]

Walz's office issued a press release after receiving Bondi's letter saying, "This is not common sense, lawful immigration enforcement. That is not what this occupation is about. And it's not what the attorney general's letter is about," [LINK: ([221]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-222]  and at a press conference on January 26, Walz said "This has nothing to do with fraud." [LINK: ([222]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-223]   [LINK: (Minnesota Secretary of State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_Secretary_of_State]   [LINK: (Steve Simon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Simon]  refused Bondi's request and called the letter "an outrageous attempt to coerce Minnesota into giving the federal government private data on millions of US Citizens in violation of state and federal law." [LINK: ([223]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-224]  Simon noted that "Attorney General Bondi knows full well that the Governor has no formal role in managing our elections or maintaining our voter registration system. She is also well aware that this specific request is the subject of active litigation with our office." [LINK: ([224]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-225]

On January 25, Donald Trump called on Congress to pass legislation to ban state and local government sanctuary policies and called on Walz, Minneapolis Mayor Jacob Frey, and all Democratic governors and mayors "to formally cooperate with the Trump Administration to enforce our Nation’s Laws, rather than resist and stoke the flames of Division, Chaos, and Violence." [LINK: ([225]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-226]  At a press conference on January 29, White House Border Czar  [LINK: (Tom Homan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_Homan]  appeared to suggest that reductions in the number of immigration officers deployed to the Twin Cities were contingent on "cooperation" from state and local government leaders. [LINK: ([226]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-227]  [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-228]  On January 26, Minnesota US District Court judge Katherine M. Menendez referenced the letter on the first day of oral arguments in the class-action lawsuit filed by Minnesota against DHS when questioning the Justice Department's lawyers. [LINK: ([215]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-AP-Ja26-216]  [LINK: ([228]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-229]

On December 17, 2025, individual plaintiffs and the  [LINK: (American Civil Liberties Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Civil_Liberties_Union]  of Minnesota filed a class-action lawsuit (Tincher et al. v. Noem et al.) [LINK: ([229]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-230]  alleging constitutional violations by federal agents participating in Operation Metro Surge. The complaint referenced events on December 9, 2025, also documented by MPR-News [LINK: ([230]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-231]  and argued that agents engaged in retaliatory arrests against observers and conducted traffic stops without reasonable suspicion, violating First and Fourth Amendment rights. [LINK: ([231]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-232]

On January 15, 2026, the  [LINK: (ACLU) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Civil_Liberties_Union]  has filed a second class-action lawsuit alleging widespread racial profiling by federal immigration agents under the surge. [LINK: ([232]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-233]  In the complaint the ACLU argues that arrests based solely on ethnic appearance or accent violate the  [LINK: (Fifth Amendment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fifth_Amendment_to_the_United_States_Constitution] 's  [LINK: (Due Process Clause) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Due_Process_Clause]  and the  [LINK: (Equal Protection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equal_Protection_Clause]   [LINK: (Clause) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equal_Protection_Clause]  as well as the prohibition against  [LINK: (arbitrary detention) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitrary_arrest_and_detention]  without  [LINK: (probable cause) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Probable_cause] .

On January 16, Minnesota US District Court judge Katherine M. Menendez issued a preliminary injunction in the first lawsuit filed by the Minnesota ACLU in December placing specific restrictions on federal agents participating in "Operation Metro Surge" in Minnesota. The ruling ordered agents not to retaliate against individuals "engaging in peaceful and unobstructive protest activity". Specifically, the court prohibited the use of pepper spray or other "crowd dispersal tools" as retaliation for protected speech and barred agents from detaining motorists who were not "forcibly obstructing or interfering with" officers. [LINK: ([233]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-234]  [LINK: ([234]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-235]  On January 21, the  [LINK: (United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Court_of_Appeals_for_the_Eighth_Circuit]  issued an  [LINK: (administrative stay) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Administrative_stay]  of the Minnesota US District Court ruling to allow for the administration to file an appeal. [LINK: ([235]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-236]  [LINK: ([236]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-237]  On January 26, it issued a formal stay to block the injunction during the appeals process, finding the injunction to be "vague and overly broad" and that parts of it were essentially orders to "obey the law". [LINK: ([237]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-238]

In a review of federal court filings in Minnesota for  [LINK: (wrongful detention) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrongful_detention]  lawsuits, the Minnesota Star Tribune found 288 cases filed from January 1 through January 21 and 344 filed from December 1 through January 21, which compared with 128 filed in 2025 in total and 375 filed between 2016 and 2024. [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-MN_Star_Tribune_1-22-2026-146]   [LINK: (Politico) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Politico]  subsequently reported that the judges of the Minneapolis US District Court have consistently ruled that the Trump administration had violated the law (sometimes egregiously), ruling in favor of the administration in only a handful of cases. [LINK: ([238]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-239]

On January 27, Minnesota US District Court Chief Judge  [LINK: (Patrick J. Schiltz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_J._Schiltz]  ordered acting ICE Director  [LINK: (Todd Lyons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Todd_Lyons]  to appear in court over the agency failing to follow dozens of court orders in the wrongful detention lawsuits, with Schiltz threatening to hold Lyons in  [LINK: (contempt of court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contempt_of_court]  for failure to do so. [LINK: ([239]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-240]  While Schiltz acknowledged in the ruling that ordering the head of an agency to appear in federal court was extraordinary, Schiltz also wrote that "the extent of ICE's violation of court orders is likewise extraordinary, and lesser measures have been tried and failed." [LINK: ([240]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-241]  The next day, Schiltz temporarily withdrew his order for Lyons to appear after the agency released a wrongfully detained person that led Schiltz to issue the order. [LINK: ([241]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-242]  [LINK: ([242]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-243]  Schiltz issued a broadside saying that ICE had violated nearly 100 court orders, and that, in January alone, ICE had disobeyed more court directives than "some federal agencies have violated in their entire existence." [LINK: ([243]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-courtorderviolationsbyice-244]  [LINK: ([244]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-245]

Also on January 27,  [LINK: (Western Texas U.S. District Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_District_Court_for_the_Western_District_of_Texas]  Judge  [LINK: (Samuel Frederick Biery Jr.) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Frederick_Biery_Jr.]  issued an order blocking the deportation of Liam Conejo Ramos, a 5-year old Ecuadorian boy, and his father,  [LINK: (who were detained in Minneapolis a week earlier) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Detention_of_Liam_Conejo_Ramos]  and removed to a family detention center near  [LINK: (San Antonio) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Antonio] , while a wrongful detention lawsuit to allow them to stay in the country proceeds. [LINK: ([245]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-246]  [LINK: ([246]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-247]


# MILITARY MOBILIZATION #

On January 7,  [LINK: (Minnesota Governor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_of_Minnesota]   [LINK: (Tim Walz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tim_Walz]  issued a  [LINK: (warning order) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operations_order#Variations]  to the  [LINK: (Minnesota National Guard) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_National_Guard]  following the  [LINK: (killing of Renée Good) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good] . [LINK: ([247]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-248]  [LINK: ([248]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-249]  The next day, Walz ordered the Minnesota National Guard to be " [LINK: (staged) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staging_area]  and  [LINK: (ready) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alert_state] "; Walz's office issued a press statement saying: "[The National Guard] remain[s] ready in the event they are needed to help keep the peace, ensure public safety, and allow for peaceful demonstrations". [LINK: ([249]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-250]  [LINK: ([250]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-251]  On January 17, the Minnesota National Guard announced that it had been mobilized but not deployed by Walz to support the  [LINK: (Minnesota State Patrol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_State_Patrol]  with activated members planning to wear  [LINK: (yellow reflective vests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-visibility_clothing]  to "help distinguish them from other agencies in similar uniforms", [LINK: ([251]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-252]  [LINK: ([252]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-NPR_1-18-2026-253]  while the  [LINK: (Minnesota Department of Public Safety) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_Department_of_Public_Safety]  stated that the Minnesota National Guard "are not deployed to city streets at this time, but are ready to help support public safety". [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-CNN_1-18-2026-254]  Following the  [LINK: (killing of Alex Pretti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Alex_Pretti]  on January 24, Walz deployed the Minnesota National Guard to assist local law enforcement at the request of the  [LINK: (Hennepin County Sheriff's Office) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hennepin_County_Sheriff%27s_Office]  and the Minneapolis city government. [LINK: ([254]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-255]  [LINK: ([255]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-256]

On January 15, President  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump]  threatened to invoke the  [LINK: (Insurrection Act of 1807) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insurrection_Act_of_1807]  in response to the  [LINK: (Renée Good protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Ren%C3%A9e_Good_protests]  in Minneapolis against ICE operations in the city, which  [LINK: (Minnesota Attorney General) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_Attorney_General]   [LINK: (Keith Ellison) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keith_Ellison]  has said he will challenge in court if Trump does so. [LINK: ([116]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-:13-117]  [LINK: ([256]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-257]  Legal scholars dispute that the conditions that permit invocation of the Insurrection Act have occurred in Minneapolis based on  [LINK: (historical precedent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_invocations_of_the_Insurrection_Act]  despite the law's facially broad language. [LINK: ([257]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-258]  [LINK: ([258]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-259]  Trump backtracked from the threat the next day, saying there was not a "reason right now" to do so but reiterated that "It's been used a lot, and if I needed it, I'd use it". [LINK: ([259]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-260]  On the same day, a  [LINK: (grand jury) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_juries_in_the_United_States]  issued subpoenas to Walz and  [LINK: (Minneapolis Mayor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_mayors_of_Minneapolis]   [LINK: (Jacob Frey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Frey]  as part of a  [LINK: (United States Department of Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Justice]  investigation of whether Walz and Frey obstructed federal immigration law enforcement through public statements. [LINK: ([260]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-261]  [LINK: ([261]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-262]  On January 20, six subpoenas were sent to the offices of Walz, Ellison, Frey,  [LINK: (Saint Paul Mayor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_mayors_of_Saint_Paul,_Minnesota]   [LINK: (Kaohly Her) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaohly_Her] , and local government officials in  [LINK: (Ramsey County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramsey_County,_Minnesota]  and  [LINK: (Hennepin County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hennepin_County,_Minnesota] . [LINK: ([262]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-263]  [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-264]  [LINK: ([264]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-265]

On January 18, the  [LINK: (United States Department of Defense) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Defense]  reportedly ordered 1,500 active-duty soldiers to prepare for a possible deployment to Minnesota, including two  [LINK: (battalions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battalion_(United_States_Army)]  from the  [LINK: (11th Airborne Division) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/11th_Airborne_Division]  of the  [LINK: (United States Army) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Army]  based in Alaska. [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-CNN_1-18-2026-254]  [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-266]  In an emailed press statement, department spokesperson  [LINK: (Sean Parnell) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sean_Parnell_(spokesperson)]  stated, "The Department of War is always prepared to execute the orders of the Commander-in-Chief if called upon", [LINK: ([252]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-NPR_1-18-2026-253]  but an unnamed Trump administration source has said that the standby order does not guarantee a deployment will occur or is imminent. [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-CNN_1-18-2026-254]  An unnamed Defense Department source has confirmed that the standby order was issued in response to Trump's threats to invoke the Insurrection Act. [LINK: ([266]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-267]  In response to the reports of the standby order, Frey said in an interview: "It's ridiculous, but we will not be intimidated by the actions of this federal government ... It is not fair, it's not just, and it's completely unconstitutional." [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-268]  On January 20, the Defense Department reportedly issued a second standby order to a  [LINK: (brigade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brigade_(United_States_Army)]  of the  [LINK: (Military Police Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_Police_Corps_(United_States)]  stationed at  [LINK: (Fort Bragg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Bragg]  in North Carolina to prepare for potential deployment to Minneapolis. [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-269]  [LINK: ([269]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-270]  [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-271]


# RESULTS #

DHS reported by December 13, 2025, the operation had resulted in the arrest of 400 undocumented immigrants, claiming this included pedophiles, rapists, kidnappers, and drug traffickers. [LINK: ([271]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-272]  In January 2026, ICE reported that 103 out of 2,000 arrestees, or about 5 percent, had records of violent crimes. [LINK: ([272]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-273]

A review of a list of names of individuals ICE said it had arrested in Minneapolis, however, showed that at least several had not in fact been arrested in the operation but had been transferred from state custody to DHS before December 1, 2025, including one individual who had been transferred in 2003. [LINK: ([273]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-274]  On January 19, 2026, DHS Secretary Kristi Noem claimed in a post on  [LINK: (Twitter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter]  that ICE had "arrested over 10,000 criminal illegal aliens" in Minneapolis, including 3,000 in the past six weeks. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-275]


# ANALYSIS #

Green Beret veteran  [LINK: (Anthony Aguilar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_Aguilar]  and American professor Chris Hughes said the violence of Metro Surge reflected the  [LINK: (imperial boomerang) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_boomerang]  theory. [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-276]  [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge#cite_note-277]


